WHAT SHIPPED
------------
1. packages/voice/src/openai-direct/{api,provider}.ts
   `OpenAIDirectProvider` implements VoiceProvider (CONTRACTS §5a) on the main
   thread, reusing the browser-cascade seams rather than forking them:
   `TutorTurnRunner`/`LlmEngine` (browser-cascade/llm-turn.ts) with a new
   fetch-backed streaming chat.completions engine, `EnergyVad`/`SpeechBuffer`
   (browser-cascade/vad.ts) for end-of-utterance, `SentenceChunker` + the
   [[reading]]/[[pace]] markers (inside the turn runner), and the AudioAdapter
   for capture and playback.
     STT  POST /v1/audio/transcriptions, gpt-4o-mini-transcribe, multipart WAV
          built from the 16 kHz PCM16 frames, `language` = ISO-639-1 of the
          learner's learningLocale.
     LLM  POST /v1/chat/completions, gpt-4o-mini, stream:true, tools =
          @sotto/core TOOL_DEFINITIONS, temperature 0.4, max_tokens 200.
          SSE parser ignores the `obfuscation` padding field.
     TTS  POST /v1/audio/speech, gpt-4o-mini-tts, response_format 'pcm',
          played with audio.playPcm(buf, 24000) — the rate is hardcoded
          because the pcm response carries no rate metadata at all (R4-B1
          phase 2). One voice per language, documented in api.ts's TTS_VOICES.
     Prompt: the same `buildSystemInstruction` the server uses.
     Tools:  the same client-side seven, over the existing `tool_call`
             VoiceEvent + `respondTool`.
     Limits: maxMs 1_200_000 / idleMs 90_000, as CONTRACTS §5b.
     Errors: 401/403 -> {code:'byok_invalid_key', recoverable:false};
             429 -> {code:'byok_rate_limited', recoverable:true};
             network/opaque -> {code:'byok_network_failed', recoverable:true}.
     Key validation: `validateOpenAIKey(key, fetch)` uses GET /v1/models,
             the only endpoint whose 401 a browser page can read.
   Realtime is NOT used (STRATEGY.md keeps the cascade); the GA-handshake
   finding from R4-B1 is noted in the provider header for a later lane.

2. apps/client/src/voice/byokKey.ts
   Web: localStorage under `sotto.byok.openaiKey`. Native: expo-secure-store,
   lazily imported. Never in the Zustand persisted store (which Profile >
   Export writes to a file), never logged, never in a URL. Exports
   get/set/remove/hasByokKey plus a sync `cachedByokKey()` for pickProvider,
   and `maskKey`, which returns `sk-••••••••` with NO key-derived characters
   (deliberately not even a tail — see the note in that file).

3. apps/client/src/voice/{availability,sessionManager}.ts
   VoicePath gains 'byok'. Ranking: phone -> cloud > byok > browser/download;
   desktop -> local > browser > byok, with byok added to `alternatives`
   wherever a chip row makes sense. `pickProvider('byok')` builds
   OpenAIDirectProvider from the stored key, falling back to the local
   provider if the key vanished between the gate and the call.

4. Settings: Profile's existing `settings.group.tutor` group gains a
   `byok.row` row (value byok.on/byok.off) -> new screen
   apps/client/app/settings/openai-key.tsx (masked input, Save with
   GET /v1/models validation, Remove, `byok.caption`, link to docs/byok.md).
   The voice screen's path chip renders `byok.pathLabel`.
   17 new keys in ALL NINE catalogs (en, fr, es, it, pt, ro, ca, zh-Hans,
   zh-Hant) with real translations; every catalog is 334 keys.

5. docs/byok.md (what it is, how to get a key, cost, what leaves the device,
   how to remove, known limits) and the README "Bring your own key" line,
   which was a forward pointer and is now the real one.

HONEST DEVIATIONS FROM THE CARD
-------------------------------
- `settings.group.tutor` already existed ("Tutor preferences") in all nine
  catalogs, so the row was added to that group rather than creating a second
  one with the same eyebrow.
- `pnpm --filter @sotto/voice test` does not exist (packages/voice has no
  `test` script; the root `vitest run` covers it). The equivalent scoped runs
  used below are `npx vitest run packages/voice` and `npx vitest run
  apps/client`.
- No i18n completeness test exists in the repo; catalog parity was checked by
  key count (all nine at 334) instead.
- The live mic turn ran on Chromium, not WebKit: WebKit has no equivalent of
  `--use-file-for-fake-audio-capture`, so a fake microphone cannot be fed to
  it. The settings half of the proof DID run on WebKit with the
  devices['iPhone 15'] descriptor, and a desktop 1440 pass was added, per the
  card's fallback clause.
- `byok.pathLabel` is used by the voice screen's chip instead of adding a
  `voice.path.byok` key, so the copy lives with the rest of the BYOK strings.

